You can use PHP's DOMDocument class to parse the HTML and extract the specific content you want. Here's an example:

```
$pageContent = file_get_contents('http://www.pullcontentshere.com/');
$doc = new DOMDocument();
$doc->loadHTML($pageContent);
$div = $doc->getElementById('myContent');
$content = $doc->saveHTML($div);
echo $content;
```

First, we load the page content into a DOMDocument object. Then, we use the `getElementById` method to select the `<div>` element we want (with the ID "myContent"). Finally, we use the `saveHTML` method to extract the HTML content of that element and save it to the `$content` variable.

Note: make sure to use `loadHTML` instead of `loadHTMLFile`, as file_get_contents returns a string, not a file.

The issue with the current code is that <code>$(this).length</code> is always 1 for radio buttons and checkboxes because they are considered as a single input element with multiple options/values. To check if any of the options have been checked, you can use the <code>:checked</code> selector along with <code>.length</code> to check if at least one option has been selected. Here's an updated code snippet:

<pre><code>valid = true;
$("#studentForm input.mustEnter, #studentForm input.mustCheck").each(function() {
    if( ($(this).hasClass('mustEnter') && $(this).val() != false ) 
     || ($(this).hasClass('mustCheck') && $(this).is(':checked')) ) {
        $(this).parent().removeClass("errorHighlight");
    } else { 
        $(this).parent().addClass('errorHighlight');
        valid = false;
    }
});
</code></pre>

With the updated code, <code>$(this).is(':checked')</code> is used to check if at least one option is checked for radio buttons and checkboxes. This should correctly highlight the error field if any mandatory field is not filled or checked.
